# Data Model — Olist (3NF, Silver Layer)

```mermaiderDiagram
  customers ||--o{ customer_orders : has
  customer_orders ||--|| orders : places
  orders ||--o{ order_items : contains
  orders ||--o{ payments : paid_by
  orders ||--o{ reviews : reviewed_by
  products ||--o{ order_items : listed_in
  sellers ||--o{ order_items : fulfills
  product_categories ||--o{ products : classifies
  geolocation ||--o{ customers : locates

  customers {
    string customer_unique_id PK
    int customer_zip_code_prefix FK
  }
  customer_orders {
    string customer_id PK
    string customer_unique_id FK
  }
  orders {
    string order_id PK
    string customer_id FK
    string order_status
    timestamp order_purchase_timestamp
    timestamp order_delivered_customer_date
    timestamp order_estimated_delivery_date
  }
  order_items {
    string order_id PK
    int order_item_id PK
    string product_id FK
    string seller_id FK
    decimal price
    decimal freight_value
  }
  payments {
    string order_id PK
    int payment_sequential PK
    string payment_type
    int payment_installments
    decimal payment_value
  }
  reviews {
    string review_id PK
    string order_id FK
    int review_score
  }
  products {
    string product_id PK
    string product_category_name FK
  }
  product_categories {
    string product_category_name PK
    string category_name_english
  }
  sellers {
    string seller_id PK
    int seller_zip_code_prefix FK
  }
```

## Normalisation reasoning

**1NF**: All columns are atomic — no comma-separated lists or repeating groups.
order_items already stores one row per item, not one row per order with a list of products.

**2NF**: No partial-key dependency. order_items has a composite key (order_id, order_item_id);
price and freight_value depend on the full key. Anything depending only on product_id
(weight, dimensions, category) lives in `products`, not repeated on every item row.

**3NF**: No transitive dependency. product_category_name's English translation depends on
the category, not the product — split into `product_categories`. City/state depend on
zip prefix, not the customer — handled via `geolocation`.

**Many-to-many resolved**: an order can have multiple payment methods, resolved via the
`payments` table keyed on (order_id, payment_sequential).

**Deliberate denormalisation**: none in silver — silver stays fully normalised.
Denormalisation happens deliberately in gold (star schema) for query performance.